{% extends 'layout.njk' %}
{% block content %}
<div class="page-heading">
  <h1>Research</h1>
  <p>My current work connects cellular mobility management with graph learning and reinforcement learning.</p>
</div>
<article>
  <header class="paper-header">
    <h2>{{ profile.paperTitle }}</h2>
    <p class="authors"><strong>Saange Tamang</strong> (first author), Sulav Kandel (co-author)<br><span class="small-note">Paschimanchal Campus, Institute of Engineering, Tribhuvan University</span></p>
    <p class="status">{{ profile.paperStatus }}<br><span class="small-note">Proceedings citation pending.</span></p>
    <div class="inline-links">
      <a href="/assets/documents/handover-study.pdf">Read paper <span class="small-note">(PDF)</span></a>
      <a href="{{ profile.code }}">Research code</a>
      <a href="/assets/documents/handover-study.bib" download>Download citation</a>
    </div>
    <div class="study-summary">
      <p><strong>Study summary.</strong> In 639 held-out Kathmandu-area simulation cases, the three learned policies achieved 8.86–9.99% higher aggregate throughput and 36.08–53.00% fewer handovers per hour than the configured A3-style baseline. Short cell stays became more common.</p>
      <p class="small-note">The comparison measures the controller as a whole under simulated conditions. The benefit of graph attention and performance on live networks remain open questions.</p>
    </div>
    <nav class="project-toc research-toc" aria-label="Research sections"><a href="#findings-heading">Findings</a><a href="#contribution-heading">My contribution</a><a href="#method-heading">Method</a><a href="#evaluation-heading">Evaluation</a><a href="#public-checks">Public code checks</a><a href="#limits-heading">Limits</a><a href="#next-study">Proposed study</a></nav>
  </header>

  <section class="section-row" aria-labelledby="question-heading">
    <h2 class="section-label" id="question-heading">The question</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>As a phone travels, the strongest nearby cell and the traffic on each cell change. Switching too often can interrupt service; staying with a weak or congested cell can reduce data delivery.</p>
      <p>We investigated whether a learned serving-cell controller could improve that balance relative to a configured LTE Event A3-style reference. The study measures service quality and switching behavior together.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-row" aria-labelledby="contribution-heading">
    <h2 class="section-label" id="contribution-heading">My contribution</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>I led the technical work for the two-author conference study, from implementation and training through evaluation and analysis. Sulav Kandel collected field observations to motivate the study, extracted site locations, and cross-checked coordinates against OpenStreetMap. We shared the manuscript writing and revision equally.</p>
      <dl class="contribution-list">
        <div><dt>Implementation &amp; training</dt><dd>I implemented the Python simulator and PyTorch controller and trained the graph-attention, dueling Double-DQN policies.<span class="artifact-links"><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/src/toponet/models/graph_q.py">Graph-attention model</a><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/src/toponet/agents/ddqn.py">Double-DQN agent</a></span></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Evaluation &amp; validation</dt><dd>I designed and ran the paired comparison of three frozen policies against a configured A3-style baseline under matched routes, radio, and traffic.<span class="artifact-links"><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/src/toponet/evaluation/replay.py">Policy replay</a><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/tests/test_core.py">Numerical checks</a></span></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Analysis</dt><dd>I analyzed paired differences in throughput and handovers and found that fewer handovers could coincide with a higher share of short stays.<span class="artifact-links"><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/src/toponet/evaluation/analysis.py">Analysis code</a><a href="{{ profile.code }}/tree/main/paper">Manuscript and figures</a></span></dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p>The paper grew from our one-year undergraduate major project in semesters 7–8. We revised that broader handover-and-load-balancing work into a focused, two-author study of handover optimization.</p>
      <p><a href="/projects/#major-project">Undergraduate project background</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-row" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
    <h2 class="section-label" id="method-heading">Method</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>We represented 46 cells as a proximity graph, with 15 features per cell describing simulated signal, load, mobility, and recent connection history. A graph-attention encoder and dueling Double-DQN model selected among feasible serving-cell actions.</p>
      <p>Three frozen trained policies were evaluated against the same configured A3-style controller. Within each comparison, the external route, radio, and traffic conditions were matched.</p>
      <figure class="figure" id="study-layout">
        <a href="/assets/images/cell-layout.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size study layout figure"><img src="/assets/images/cell-layout.webp" width="{{ sizes['cell-layout'].width }}" height="{{ sizes['cell-layout'].height }}" alt="Two panels show the 46 study locations and 96 proximity links. Node colors indicate neighbor counts, not radio quality." loading="lazy"></a>
        <figcaption>The study’s site-as-cell layout and candidate-neighbor graph. Links connect locations within 1.5 km; these are proximity links rather than a verified operator neighbor list.<span class="attribution">Map data © <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap contributors</a> (ODbL). Figure from the manuscript.</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-row" aria-labelledby="evaluation-heading">
    <h2 class="section-label" id="evaluation-heading">Evaluation</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>The main evaluation covered <strong>639 reserved Kathmandu-area cases</strong> and <strong>540 Pokhara route–layout–condition groups</strong>. The transfer groups combine 60 routes, three synthetic cell layouts, and three condition pairs.</p>
      <p>We assessed aggregate throughput, handovers, ping-pongs, weak-service time, and short completed cell stays. Paired 95% cluster-bootstrap intervals used 2,000 resamples, grouping by origin–destination pair or route.</p>
      <figure class="figure">
        <a href="/assets/images/evaluation-protocol.webp" aria-label="Open the full-size evaluation protocol"><img src="/assets/images/evaluation-protocol.webp" width="{{ sizes['evaluation-protocol'].width }}" height="{{ sizes['evaluation-protocol'].height }}" alt="Protocol diagram describing the training, development, reserved-route and Pokhara transfer sets, including overlap disclosures." loading="lazy"></a>
        <figcaption>Data partitions and transfer design from the paper. Reserved route IDs do not imply geographically disjoint roads.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-row" aria-labelledby="findings-heading">
    <h2 class="section-label" id="findings-heading">Findings</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <section class="table-wrap" aria-label="Reported simulation outcomes" tabindex="0">
        <table>
          <caption>Aggregate changes relative to the configured A3-style reference. Ranges span the three frozen policies.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Evaluation</th><th scope="col">Throughput</th><th scope="col">Handovers/hour</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Kathmandu-area<br><span class="small-note">639 reserved cases</span></th><td class="num">8.86–9.99% higher</td><td class="num">36.08–53.00% fewer</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Pokhara transfer<br><span class="small-note">540 scenario groups</span></th><td class="num">3.66–3.85% higher</td><td class="num">23.68–47.10% fewer</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
      <p>All three policies delivered higher aggregate throughput and fewer handovers in these simulations. They also had fewer ping-pongs and less weak-service time. However, the proportion of short completed stays increased for all three policies on the reserved routes; transfer results varied by policy.</p>
      <figure class="figure">
        <a href="/assets/documents/paired-primary-outcomes.pdf" aria-label="Open the original paired-outcomes figure PDF"><img src="/assets/images/paired-outcomes.webp" width="{{ sizes['paired-outcomes'].width }}" height="{{ sizes['paired-outcomes'].height }}" alt="Paired throughput, handover and ping-pong differences for training seeds 17, 27 and 37, with 95 percent cluster-bootstrap intervals, across same-area and Pokhara tests." loading="lazy"></a>
        <figcaption>Paired differences from A3, with 95% cluster-bootstrap intervals. The intervals describe the sampled scenarios and frozen policies; they do not account for all variation from retraining or testing other cities.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-row" aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
    <h2 class="section-label" id="limits-heading">Code, data &amp; limits</h2>
    <div class="section-body">
      <p>Nepal Telecom drive-test logs were unavailable for research use. The highway dashboard observations helped motivate the study. Kathmandu used mapped cell-site locations and Pokhara used synthetic cell layouts. Both evaluations used real road geometry with simulated radio and traffic.</p>
      <p>These are simulation findings. The study uses simplified radio and resource-sharing models, three training seeds, some shared roads between training and reserved routes, and simulator-provided information. Pokhara uses real road geometry with synthetic cell layouts.</p>
      <p>The comparison does not establish live-network performance or isolate the benefit of graph attention. A later-added load-aware rule is an exploratory diagnostic, separate from the main configured reference comparison.</p>
      <p>The public repository contains code, configurations, numerical checks, and the manuscript. Full reproduction of the reported results additionally requires route/layout inputs, frozen checkpoints, and complete case records that are outside the public release.</p>
      <div class="inline-links"><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/docs/RESULTS.md">Results and limitations</a><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/docs/DATA_AND_REPRODUCIBILITY.md">Data and reproduction details</a><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md">Implementation guide</a><a href="{{ profile.code }}/blob/main/tests/test_core.py">Public code checks</a></div>
      <h3 id="public-checks">Run the public code checks</h3>
      <p>The public repository includes numerical checks covering the controller, simulator, and baseline behavior. After <a href="{{ profile.code }}#install">installing the package</a>, run this from the repository root:</p>
      <pre><code>python -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_core.py' -v</code></pre>
      <p class="small-note">These checks exercise the implementation with small inputs. Reproducing the paper’s reported results requires the additional experiment assets listed above.</p>
